name: searching text
boss: Murmur
flavor: I decide what your commands get to read.
brief: grep prints the lines that match a pattern. It is the single most
brief: useful command on this system, and most of its power is in six flags:
brief: -i ignore case, -v invert, -n number, -c count, -l name files, -r walk
brief: a tree.
brief:
brief: Patterns are regular expressions unless you say otherwise. -F turns
brief: that off and matches the text literally, which is what you want the
brief: moment your pattern contains a dot or a bracket you meant as itself.
---
q: {%show% every line of @log@ containing @sev@|which lines of @log@ mention @sev@?|pull the @sev@ lines out of @log@}
a: grep @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep
hint: just grep, the pattern, then the file
---
q: {%show% the @sev@ lines in @log@, ignoring case|find @sev@ in @log@ whether it is upper or lower case|match @sev@ in @log@ case-insensitively}
a: grep -i @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -i
hint: i for case-insensitive
---
q: {%show% every line of @log@ that does NOT contain @sev@|which lines of @log@ are missing @sev@?|drop the @sev@ lines from @log@ and show the rest}
a: grep -v @sev@ @log@
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -v
hint: v for invert
---
q: {%count% how many lines of @log@ contain @sev@|how many @log@ lines mention @sev@?|report the number of @sev@ lines in @log@}
a: grep -c @sev@ @log@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -c
hint: this counts LINES, not occurrences -- two matches on one line count once
---
q: {%show% the @sev@ lines of @log@ with their line numbers|number the @sev@ matches in @log@|which line numbers in @log@ have @sev@?}
a: grep -n @sev@ @log@
out: ^[0-9]+:
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -n
hint: n prefixes each match with its line number
---
q: {search every file under here for @term@, recursively|%find% @term@ anywhere in the tree below you|grep the whole directory tree for @term@}
a: grep -r @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -r
hint: r walks into subdirectories; the dot is where it starts
---
q: %list% only the NAMES of files under here that contain @term@
a: grep -rl @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -l
hint: l switches grep from printing lines to printing filenames
---
q: %list% the files under here that do NOT contain @term@
a: grep -rL @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -L
hint: capital L is the opposite of -l -- files with NO match
---
q: %show% only the matched text from @log@, not the whole line
a: grep -o @sev@ @log@
out: ^@sev@$
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: o prints just the matched substring, one per line
---
q: %show% lines of @log@ where @sev@ appears as a whole word, not inside another
a: grep -w @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -w
hint: w anchors the match to word boundaries
---
q: %show% lines of @log@ that consist of exactly @sev@ and nothing else
a: grep -x @sev@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -x
hint: x requires the whole line to match, not just part of it
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show the 2 lines after each match
a: grep -A 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -A
hint: A for after, followed by the count
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show the 2 lines before each match
a: grep -B 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -B
hint: B for before, followed by the count
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show 2 lines of context on either side
a: grep -C 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -C
hint: C for context -- both before and after
---
q: %show% only the first 2 matching lines of @log@ and stop
a: grep -m 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -m
hint: m caps the number of matches before grep stops reading
---
q: {search @log@ for the literal text 0.0.0.0 -- the dots must not be wildcards|find the literal string 0.0.0.0 in @log@, not the dots-as-wildcards version|search @log@ for 0.0.0.0 as plain text, no regex}
a: grep -F 0.0.0.0 @log@
out: 0\.0\.0\.0
ref: grep
teach: grep -F
hint: F for fixed string
---
q: %find% lines in @log@ holding an IP-like number, using extended regex
a: grep -E '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' @log@
out: [0-9]
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -E
hint: E turns on extended regex, so + doesn't need a backslash
---
q: {match either @sev@ or @term@ in @log@ in one extended-regex pass|find lines in @log@ containing @sev@ or @term@, using one extended-regex command|search @log@ for @sev@ or @term@ with a single extended-regex pattern}
a: grep -E '@sev@|@term@' @log@
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -E
hint: the pipe inside the pattern means OR; -E is what lets you write it unescaped
---
q: {check quietly whether @log@ mentions @sev@ -- no output, just an exit status|silently check whether @sev@ appears in @log@, using only the exit status|test whether @log@ contains @sev@ without printing anything}
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: this is the form you use inside an if
---
q: {search @log@ for @sev@ and suppress any error about unreadable files|search @log@ for @sev@, silencing any complaint about files it can't read|search @log@ for @sev@ without printing errors for unreadable files}
a: grep -s @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -s
hint: s silences the "no such file" complaints, built into grep itself
---
q: {search @log@ using each line of @txt@ as a separate pattern|search @log@ against a whole list of patterns, one per line of @txt@|search @log@ for anything matching a line from @txt@}
a: grep -f @txt@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -f
hint: f reads patterns from a file instead of the command line, one per line
---
q: {search @log@ for @sev@ naming the pattern explicitly with a flag|search @log@ for @sev@, passing the pattern via its own flag|search @log@ for @sev@ using -e to name the pattern}
a: grep -e @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -e
hint: -e is how you search for something starting with a dash
